---
// The single site-wide statement of method. Every ranked guide carries a short
// topic-specific block - its own counts, source types and known weakness - and
// links here for the argument. Stating it once means it can be stated properly,
// and gives us one page to point at when a ranking is challenged.
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How we rank"
  description="How London Travel Geek builds its ranked guides: counting independent sources rather than publishing opinions, how sources are weighted, and what the method cannot tell you."
>
  <article class="legal container">
    <p class="eyebrow">London Travel Geek</p>
    <h1>How we rank</h1>
    <p class="legal__intro">
      Our guides are not a list of places we liked. Every venue is judged on how
      many <strong>independent</strong> sources name it — awards, critics,
      specialists and the people who actually went and filmed it — counted, not
      asserted. This page explains how that count is built, and where it fails.
    </p>

    <h2>Why counting rather than reviewing</h2>
    <p>
      A single enthusiastic review is one person on one night. It might be the
      chef's best service of the year or their worst. What it cannot tell you is
      whether a kitchen is reliably good, and that is the only thing worth
      knowing before you travel across a city to eat somewhere.
    </p>
    <p>
      So we invert it. Instead of adding one more opinion, we record who has
      already published one, and count the places that separate judges keep
      arriving at independently. Ten outlets converging on the same address is
      evidence in a way that any one of them alone is not.
    </p>
    <p>
      The corpus behind this currently holds <strong>517 source records across
      48 topics, carrying 9,206 citations</strong> drawn from
      <strong>227 registered publications, channels and award bodies</strong>.
      Of the venues named in it, 1,020 are named by two or more independent
      sources and 405 carry a dated award.
    </p>

    <h2>The source tiers</h2>
    <p>
      Not every mention is worth the same. Sources are registered in advance and
      tiered by what kind of judgement they represent, so an unknown source is
      visible rather than silently weighted:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A — a judged, dated ranking.</strong> A named panel or
        inspectorate published a ranked or starred list with a year attached:
        50 Top Pizza, the National Pizza Awards, the Michelin guide. The
        strongest single signal, because it is falsifiable and re-run annually.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>B — an editorial publication.</strong> A masthead with editors
        paying writers to visit. Broad and useful, but over-represented, and its
        members read each other.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>C — an independent blog or specialist.</strong> One or two
        people writing from their own visits, or a single-subject specialist.
        Often earlier and more specific than the publications, and much closer
        to what a local would actually say.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>D — video.</strong> YouTube and TikTok creators who went, filmed
        and rated. The tier is the medium, not the audience: a channel with two
        million views and one with twenty thousand are both D.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>E — community.</strong> Reddit threads and forum posts where
        named people argue about where to eat. Counted per thread, never per
        comment, so one busy thread cannot outweigh a publication.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>F — the venue's own website.</strong> Used to check an address,
        a price or which branches are open. <strong>Never</strong> counted
        toward consensus — a restaurant recommending itself is not evidence.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Awards weigh more than press; press weighs more than creators. A venue
      needs support from two or more sources across two or more tiers, or one
      tier-A award, before we treat it as properly established. Five publications
      agreeing is weaker than a publication, a blogger and a video agreeing,
      because the publications read each other and the three strangers do not.
    </p>

    <h2>The counting rules</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Support is counted in distinct domains, never in URLs.</strong>
        Two lists from one publication is one opinion. Time Out's ranked guide,
        its video channel and its news desk are all Time Out, and count once.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Syndication credits the original.</strong> Where one outlet
        reports another's ranking — "X has been crowned by the Telegraph" — the
        source is the Telegraph. Counting both would let a single judgement pose
        as two.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Aggregators are excluded entirely.</strong> TripAdvisor, Yelp,
        OpenTable, Wanderlog and the rest rank by user volume, repeat each other
        and dominate search results. One crowd-sourced ranking can otherwise
        masquerade as many independent opinions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Google ratings are tracked separately</strong> and never form
        part of a placement. Review volume measures footfall, not cooking.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Age is recorded.</strong> A mention older than three years still
        counts but is flagged; a venue with nothing inside two years is treated
        as a closure candidate and re-checked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Closed venues are dropped</strong> even while live guides still
        carry them — published lists are slow to update and some never do, so the
        longer a place has been shut, the better supported it can look.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why there is no score</h2>
    <p>
      We could turn all of this into a single number out of ten. We deliberately
      do not, because the weighting behind it — how much more an award is worth
      than a magazine, a magazine than a video — is a judgement call, and a
      decimal point would imply a precision the method does not have. A score
      invites the question "how was that calculated?" and the honest answer is
      "by a person deciding".
    </p>
    <p>
      So we publish the evidence rather than the verdict: how many independent
      sources named a venue, and where it placed in the lists that actually
      published an order. You can disagree with our reading and still use the
      count.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the same reason, a rank is only ever shown against a source that
      published one. Plenty of respected guides are deliberately unordered — The
      Infatuation's best-of guides and Country & Town House both are, and one
      widely shared social list says "in no particular order" in its own caption.
      Attaching a position to any of those would be inventing it.
    </p>

    <h2>What this method cannot tell you</h2>
    <p>
      Counting sources measures agreement, not quality, and the two come apart in
      predictable ways:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>It rewards being written about.</strong> A superb neighbourhood
        restaurant that no critic has reached will score badly against a
        mediocre one with a publicist. Central and east London are covered far
        more heavily than the outer boroughs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Some categories have no judged award at all.</strong> There is no
        inspectorate for the full English breakfast or the Sunday roast. Those
        rankings rest on editorial consensus alone, which is a weaker foundation,
        and the guide says so where it applies.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Primary sources are sometimes unreadable.</strong> The National
        Pizza Awards publish their winners as images and their previous-winners
        page stops at 2024, so the 2025 results are only available through Time
        Out's reporting. We attribute those to the awards — they are the
        originating judgement — but the link cannot be checked against a primary
        page, and we say so in the guide rather than hiding it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One venue can be recorded under several names.</strong> Sources
        spell things differently and residencies get filed under their host pub.
        We merge these deliberately and keep a written note of every merge, but
        a merge is a judgement and occasionally it will be the wrong one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It is a snapshot.</strong> Restaurants close, chefs leave and
        residencies end faster than guides update. Every ranked page carries the
        date its evidence was last rebuilt.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Independence</h2>
    <p>
      No placement on this site is paid for, and no venue can buy its way into a
      guide or out of one. Where a page carries affiliate or partner links they
      are marked as such, and they play no part in what is ranked or how.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      If a ranking here looks wrong, the useful thing to tell us is which source
      we have missed, miscounted or double-counted — that is a claim we can
      check and fix, and the count will change with it.
    </p>

    <p><a href="/">Return to the London Travel Geek homepage</a></p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

